   144	
   145	Stateful cells are 15 runs (3 seeds × 5); the rest, 24. Haiku's Stateful needed a third
   146	parser version: the second still dropped the patches Haiku writes in Markdown
   147	(`**StateUpdate:**` followed by a code block) — 731 of 3,000 at T=200, in 7 of 15
   148	episodes — which an adversarial review found (§6, item 7). The cells above use the third,
   149	which accepts 2,998 of 3,000 responses at T=200 and 749 of 750 at T=50; of those, five are
   150	empty patches (`{}`, nothing to change), so 2,995 and 747 change the state. The three it
   151	does not accept contain no object at all.
   152	
   153	ReAct is flat on Haiku: −0.005 between T=50 and T=200, against −0.023 on Gemini and
   154	−0.140 in the paper over the same interval. The full-history arm the paper shows
   155	collapsing degrades on Haiku less than SKILL.state (−0.041); only Stateful degrades less.
   156	
   157	**SKILL.state fails on Haiku, and the adjudication says where.** We replay the
   158	environment with the actions the model executed and compare, step by step, the inventory
   159	the model believes (SKU, units and lot per shelf) against the real one
   160	(`experiments/adjudicar_estado.py`). A failure is attributed to the state only when the
   161	believed inventory prescribes a **different** action than the real one and the model
   162	executed exactly the action the believed inventory prescribes. "The belief matches
   163	reality" means agreement on every field the model wrote; a field it left unspecified —
   164	a shelf recorded only by its SKU, say — is not counted as a discrepancy. Of the 201
   165	failures at T=200:
   166	
   167	- **167 are caused by the state** in that sense; **17** occur while the belief differed
   168	  from reality but the discrepancy does not explain them (both inventories prescribe the
   169	  same action, or the model followed neither); **17** occur with a correct state.
   170	- **In all 17 episodes where the belief departed from reality, the origin is the same:
   171	  a `Move` executed correctly with a state patch written wrong.** `Move` is the only
   172	  transition that requires copying one shelf's contents into another key of the state,
   173	  and that copy is where it breaks — the model writes the contents of a different shelf,
   174	  the lot of the pallet it stored one step earlier, or the wrong number of units.
   175	- **Only in 7 of those 17 did the error reach a decision, all in seed 2.** In 6 of its
   176	  8 runs the corruption happens at the same step (132), after which the model ships from
   177	  a shelf that does not hold the SKU, cascades through ~25 state-caused failures and does
   178	  not correct its state even when the environment rejects the action. In seed 0 the
   179	  corrupted field is a lot number that no later decision reads: 8 corrupted runs, no
   180	  failure caused by it.
   181	
   182	What SKILL.state removes is the need to **reconstruct** the state from history. What it
   183	introduces is the need to **write** it correctly at every transition, and nothing in the
   184	runtime checks that the write matches what the action did. Gemini's belief never departed
   185	from reality in any of its 75 episodes; Haiku wrote at least one wrong patch in 17 of 24
   186	at T=200, and in 7 of them the error reached a decision. The paper's thesis survives in
   187	direction — SKILL.state is still the best arm on Gemini and within 0.02 of ReAct on
   188	Haiku — but "explicit state does not degrade" is a property of the model as much as of
   189	the runtime.
   190	
   191	**Stateful orders the two models in opposite directions.** At T=200 Stateful is the best
   192	arm on Haiku (0.999, above SKILL.state's 0.958) and, on Gemini, is indistinguishable
   193	from ReAct (0.930 against 0.913) and well below SKILL.state (1.000). The same
   194	adjudication describes both — it describes, it does not separate causes, because
   195	Stateful and SKILL.state also differ in response format, parsing, retries and prompt
   196	construction, not only in carrying the history:
   197	
   198	- **On Haiku, Stateful makes the same kind of write error as SKILL.state, far less
   199	  consequentially, and loses nothing to it.** Its belief departs from reality in 8 of 15
   200	  episodes at T=200 — always a correct action with a miswritten patch, the same failure
   201	  as above — but the wrong state prescribes a different action on only 4 steps (against
   202	  168 for SKILL.state), and on all 4 the model does what reality requires. **None** of
   203	  its 2 failures is caused by the state; both happen with a correct one.
   204	- **On Gemini it is the other way round.** Its belief departs from reality in 11 of 15
   205	  episodes at T=200 (5 from a miswritten patch, 6 from a wrong action the state did not
   206	  reflect), where SKILL.state on the same model never did in 75. Of its 211 failures, 86
   207	  are caused by the state, 48 occur with a discrepancy that does not explain them, and
   208	  **77 happen with a correct state in front of it**.
   209	
   210	A hypothesis for why fixing the parser changed so little, which we have not tested: in
   211	Stateful the model's responses go into the history, and each contains its own
   212	`StateUpdate: {...}`. The transcript therefore carried a state written by the model
   213	whether or not the runtime applied it, so the defective arm measured "state kept only in
   214	the transcript" rather than "no state".
   215	
   216	**Memory on Haiku runs into our summariser cap, and a control bounds — but does not
   217	establish — what that costs it.** The summariser has its own output cap (1,200 tokens, with an instruction
   218	to stay under 400 words). Gemini never reaches it — 0 truncated summaries in 30 episodes.
   219	Haiku overruns it constantly: 194 truncated summaries in the 24 episodes at T=50 and
   220	**2,386 at T=200**, about half of all summaries. A control arm at T=50 with the cap
   221	raised to 4,096 (3 seeds × 3, `--summary-max-tokens`) truncates nothing and scores
   222	**0.889 ± 0.126 (n=9) against 0.809 ± 0.184 (n=24)**: +0.080, Welch 95 % interval
   223	−0.037 to +0.197. The control removes the truncation and raises the observed mean, but
   224	does not establish an effect on the score, and raising the cap also changes how long a
   225	summary may be, not only whether the same summary is cut. Uncut, Memory still stays well
   226	below ReAct (0.978). Haiku's Memory column is therefore read as *our summariser with a cap one
   227	model respects and the other does not*, and we draw no cross-model conclusion from it.
