   431	### L2: retroactive invalidation
   432	
   433	An announced fact stops being true later, and the agent has to act on the correction.
   434	In the three scenarios used here (seeds 4, 10 and 6) a correct trajectory has **exactly
   435	one step the correction decides** — a property of these scenarios, not of the generator,
   436	which in other seeds yields none or several; once an
   437	agent misses it, its world diverges and further steps start to disagree with the truth,
   438	so counting them scores the cascade, not the correction. The measure is therefore per
   439	episode: does the agent get the decisive step right? All three models at T=50, seeds 4,
   440	10 and 6 × 8 repetitions, output cap 8192 (Gemini with reasoning budget 0), Claude via
   441	Microsoft Foundry and Gemini via Vertex:
   442	
   443	| Model | ReAct applies the correction | SKILL.state |
   444	|---|---|---|
   445	| Haiku 4.5 | 1/24 | **24/24** |
   446	| Sonnet 5 | 9/24 | **22/24** |
   447	| Gemini 3 Flash | 0/24 | **24/24** |
   448	
   449	**Explicit state applies the correction in 70 of 72 episodes (97 %, Wilson 95 % interval
   450	90–99 %); full history in 10 of 72 (14 %, 8–24 %).** Every episode had its decisive step,
   451	and no response was truncated at it — seven Sonnet/ReAct episodes truncated elsewhere,
   452	and none was excluded. The misses are not other errors: every failed decisive step is
   453	exactly the action of an agent that never heard the correction. The successes
   454	concentrate by scenario rather than by trial — Sonnet with ReAct scores 2/8, 0/8 and
   455	7/8 across the three seeds, and its two SKILL.state misses are both in seed 4 — so the
   456	binomial intervals understate the uncertainty of generalising to new scenarios.
   457	
   458	> **This replaces an earlier measurement that did not measure what it published.** The
   459	> previous version of this section reported "93 of 93" and "132 of 132" dependent steps
   460	> with explicit state. Its scorer took the dependent steps from the trajectory of a
   461	> *simulated* deaf agent, counted successes as dependent steps minus deaf actions without
   462	> deducting steps with no action or with another error, and its runner applied the
   463	> *correct* action whenever the runtime returned none. A runtime that never answers
   464	> scored 100 % (§6, item 11). The direction survives; the perfection does not.
   465	
   466	### Across the three models
   467	
   468	Gemini with ReAct never applies either correction — 0 of 72 in L1 without a reminder, 0
   469	of 24 in L2 — while scoring 0.913 on the long procedure; Haiku scores 0.974 at T=200 and
   470	applies the L2 correction once in 24. What holds on all three models is that **the

